  "outstanding_asks": [
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "The exemption checks the parent, but `_declared_value` resolves the var by MRO. The two rules disagree:\n\n```python\nclass Typing(BaseState, mixin=True):\n    count: int\n\nclass Value(BaseState, mixin=True):\n    count: int = 3\n\nclass S(Typing, Value, BaseState): ...   # raises MixinVarNameConflictError\n\nclass P(BaseState):\n    count: int = 1\n\nclass S(Typing, Value, P): ...           # accepted, owner=S, default=3\n```\n\nIn the second case the parent var permits the combination, but `Typing` does not use it. `Typing` gets the separate var of `Value`. Please base the exemption on the declaration that `_declared_value` returns. For example, a mixin var without a default does not conflict when a declaration with a default exists further along the MRO.",
      "addressed": false,
      "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/vars/base.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "`BaseVarShadowsInheritedVarError` shipped in reflex-base 0.9.12, and `reflex.utils.exceptions` re-exports it. Code that catches it now fails with `ImportError`. Please keep a deprecated alias for one release, as CLAUDE.md requires a fallback path.",
      "addressed": false,
      "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/utils/exceptions.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "Two mixins with the same var work on `main` today, and the MRO selects one. This line raises at once. CLAUDE.md asks for a `console.deprecate` warning first and an error in a later release. If 0.10.0 is an agreed break for this, please state that in the PR. Otherwise warn here for one release.",
      "addressed": false,
      "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/vars/base.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "This returns the parent field without a type check. With `count: str = \"a\"` on the parent and `count: int` on the mixin, the mixin now uses the `str` var. On `main` it got its own `int` var. Mixin code such as `self.count *= 2` then gives `\"aa\"`. Please raise or warn when the annotated types differ.",
      "addressed": false,
      "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/vars/base.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "This loop calls `_restates_type` two times for each step. This shorter loop has the same behavior:\n\n```python\nfound = MISSING\nfor klass in lookup_order:\n    if name not in klass.__dict__:\n        continue\n    value = klass.__dict__[name]\n    if not _restates_type(value):\n        return value if found is MISSING or isinstance(value, Field) else found\n    if found is MISSING:\n        found = value\nreturn found\n```",
      "addressed": false,
      "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/vars/base.py",
      "outdated": false
    }
